@meta
id: 03-07
title: Four doors into the same file
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-I, insert-A, open-o, open-O
par: 110
minutes: 6
vscode: four clicks, four bursts of typing
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
Four small changes to one type, and each one wants a different door into
insert mode. Working out which door before you press anything is the whole
exercise.

  1. A comment above the type, on a new first line:

         // Domain entity. The Prisma record is a different shape.

  2. `id` is missing its semicolon.

  3. `id` should be `readonly`.

  4. A `placedAt: Date;` field, directly under `customerId`.

Nothing here needs you to move along a line, and nothing here needs Enter.

@keys
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank character |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `o` | open a line below |
| `O` | open a line above |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
Four edits, four keys, in the order they appear in the file. Ask of each
one: start of the code, end of the line, a new line under, or a new line
over?
--
The comment is `O` on line one. The semicolon is `A`. The `readonly` is
`I`. The new field is `o`. The only thing left to decide is the order, and
top to bottom means you only ever press `j`.
--
Do the comment first, and remember it pushes everything down by a line, so
the `id` line is two `j` away from where you end up, not one. Do both `id`
edits without leaving that line: `I readonly ` `Esc` then `A;` `Esc`.

@solution
A reasonable run, top to bottom:

    O // Domain entity. The Prisma record is a different shape. <Esc>
    j j I readonly<Space> <Esc> A ; <Esc>
    j o placedAt: Date; <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Ninety-seven keystrokes, eighty-two of them the text you were asked for.

The thing worth taking away is that you never positioned the cursor
horizontally. Not once. Four edits at four different columns — column 1,
the end of a line, the first non-blank, and a column on a line that did
not exist yet — and no `h`, no `l`, no `$`, no `0`. Each capital carried
its own idea of where the edit belonged.

That is the difference between "move the cursor, then type", which is what
a mouse teaches you, and "say where the text goes", which is what vim is
for. Later chapters extend the same idea to whole words, sentences and
blocks, but the shape never changes.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
};

@expected order.entity.ts
// Domain entity. The Prisma record is a different shape.
export type Order = {
  readonly id: string;
  customerId: string;
  placedAt: Date;
  status: OrderStatus;
};
